@page "/Home"

<PageTitle>OSDC Cluster</PageTitle>

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Cluster</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages Cluster records: the drilling locations that group one or more well slots, their Field
    and Rig associations, well slots and their positions, identity and feature assignments, and portable versioned
    backups. It persists to SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on every mutation, and exposes the same
    operations through a REST API, an MCP server, and a Blazor web application, so a cluster created or edited in one
    interface is immediately visible through the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a Cluster</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>Cluster</b> is the physical drilling location shared by one or more wellbores: an offshore
                platform, a subsea template, an onshore pad, or a single-well site. A Cluster record carries a name,
                a description, server-owned creation and modification timestamps, an optional reference point and
                depth information describing its physical setting, and the well <b>slots</b> laid out at that
                location — while trajectory geometry, casing, and survey stations live in the Well and WellBore
                microservices, and licence/reservoir context lives in Field.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Cluster UUIDs are caller-generated so that consuming systems can mint and reference an identifier
                before the record exists on the server, which matters when a location is planned before any slot is
                spudded.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Clusters and Fields</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A Cluster can reference the <b>Field</b> it belongs to through an optional <code>FieldID</code> — an
                external UUID this service validates but does not own. A field is the geographic and administrative
                area that groups the wells, wellbores, clusters, and other installations sharing one reservoir,
                licence, or development project; a Cluster is one physical location within that field where slots and
                the wellbores drilled from them are actually positioned.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Because of that link, cluster survey runs and trajectories can be reviewed together with the parent
                field's delineation lines and calculated boundaries on the
                <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterTrajectories">Survey Display</MudLink> pages, and the
                <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink> contextual page gives read access to the field
                record itself without leaving this application.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Types of clusters: single-well and true clusters</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                The <code>IsSingleWell</code> flag distinguishes a <b>single-well virtual cluster</b> — a location
                that exists to carry exactly one slot for a standalone well, drilled without sharing the site with
                other wells — from a <b>true cluster</b>, a location deliberately laid out with multiple slots so
                several wellbores can be drilled from the same platform, template, or pad. Nothing in the data model
                caps the number of slots a Cluster can hold; the flag records the operational intent behind the
                location rather than counting entries in its slot dictionary.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                A second, independent flag, <code>IsFixedPlatform</code>, records whether the cluster sits on a fixed
                structure rather than a floating or moveable rig. Both flags are filterable through dedicated REST,
                MCP, and web-app queries — <code>GetAllSingleWell</code> and <code>GetAllFixedPlatform</code> — and
                are tracked separately in <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/StatisticsCluster">Usage Statistics</MudLink>.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Slots, position, and depth</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Each well <b>Slot</b> has its own UUID, name, description, timestamps, a latitude/longitude position
                in the WGS84 datum, and its own feature assignments — the slot is where an individual wellbore
                actually spuds. The Cluster's optional <code>ReferencePoint</code> locates the location itself in SI
                and WGS84 references, while <code>GroundMudLineDepth</code> and <code>TopWaterDepth</code> describe
                its vertical environment (seabed or ground level, and, offshore, the water surface).
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Slots are stored in a dictionary keyed by their own <code>ID</code>; the service rejects a write where
                a dictionary key does not match its slot's embedded <code>ID</code>, keeping the layout internally
                consistent. Slots are added, positioned, and assigned features from the
                <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink> page.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Identities and features</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Every cluster can carry any number of user-defined <b>identities</b> — symbolic references such as an
        operator platform name or a regulatory ID — each with a cluster-specific value, any number of <b>cluster
        feature</b> assignments describing the location as a whole, and any number of <b>slot feature</b>
        assignments describing an individual slot. Feature categories, on both clusters and slots, can be marked
        exclusive, so only one option from the category applies at a time, and optionally time-bounded, so an
        assignment carries a validity period.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The catalogs are managed on <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterIdentities">Cluster Identities</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterFeatures">Cluster Features</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/SlotFeatures">Slot Features</MudLink>, while a specific cluster's or slot's
        assignments are set from the <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink> page — a category,
        option, or identity definition cannot be deleted while a cluster or slot still references it, and removing an
        option from a category is likewise rejected while any assignment still uses it.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Conventions this microservice follows</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Cluster follows the same conventions as the other OSDC drilling microservices, so behavior learned on one
        transfers directly to the others. Resource identifiers are caller-generated UUIDs rather than server sequence
        numbers. Every mutation is checked with <b>optimistic concurrency</b>: an update or delete must echo back the
        <code>LastModificationDate</code> from the caller's latest read, and a stale request is rejected with HTTP
        409 rather than silently overwriting a concurrent change. Updates are full replacements — a PUT must include
        everything that should remain stored, including the complete <code>Slots</code> dictionary, not just the
        changed fields.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Cluster-owned catalogs — identities, cluster features, and slot features — are validated on every write: a
        cluster cannot reference a category, option, or definition that does not exist, and none of those catalog
        entries can be deleted, nor can an option be removed from a category, while any cluster or slot still
        references it. The external <code>FieldID</code> and <code>RigID</code> references are validated against the
        Field and Rig services when those are reachable, but a cluster write is never blocked solely because one of
        those services is temporarily unavailable.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink> page, export <b>all</b>
        clusters or an ordered <b>selection</b> into one versioned document that carries the complete clusters
        together with only the identity, cluster-feature, and slot-feature catalog entries they actually reference,
        plus a verified UUID-to-name manifest for the Field and Rig records they point at — enough to reproduce the
        clusters on another installation without pulling in unrelated catalog clutter.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring the same document back offers a <b>conflict policy</b> — fail if any cluster UUID already exists,
        or replace matching clusters while creating the rest — and a <b>catalog policy</b> — map onto existing local
        catalog entries by UUID or normalized name, or additionally create the missing ones locally with
        server-generated UUIDs. Validation, catalog mapping, and every cluster write happen inside one SQLite
        transaction, so a restore of many clusters either commits completely or leaves the database untouched.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <b>Cluster Management</b> menu is the primary workflow: open
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink> to browse, search, create, edit, and remove
        clusters, lay out and position their slots, and manage identity and feature assignments. Manage the shared
        catalogs on <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterIdentities">Cluster Identities</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterFeatures">Cluster Features</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/SlotFeatures">Slot Features</MudLink>, and move data between installations on
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterBackupRestore">Cluster Backup and Restore</MudLink>.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <b>Survey Display</b> menu shows <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterTrajectories">Cluster
        Trajectories</MudLink> and <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ClusterSurveyRuns">Cluster Survey Runs</MudLink> in
        3D and horizontal projection, with optional uncertainty ellipses and, when the cluster belongs to a field,
        that field's delineation lines and calculated boundaries overlaid for context. The <b>Contextual Data</b>
        menu gives read access to <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Rig">Rig</MudLink>, and the
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/ProjectionDefinition">Cartographic Projection</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/GeodeticDatum">Geodetic Datum</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/Spheroid">Spheroid</MudLink> definitions a cluster's coordinate conversions
        rely on, each backed by its own microservice and shown here through reusable Razor components.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Calculators</b> menu exposes the same synchronous, stateless evaluations used elsewhere in OSDC —
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/FieldCartographicConverter">Cartographic Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumCalculation">Vertical Datum Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/EarthGravityCalculation">Earth Gravity Evaluation</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldCalculation">Earth Magnetic Field Evaluation</MudLink> — for
        one-off conversions and lookups while working a cluster, and the <b>Diagnostics</b> menu's
        <MudLink Href="/Cluster/webapp/StatisticsCluster">Usage Statistics</MudLink> page reports per-endpoint request
        counts for this service replica. All numeric fields follow the unit system selected for the page through the
        OSDC unit-reference system.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same cluster data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store, so it can
        be managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring clusters and slots, inspecting contextual data, running calculators, and reviewing
                    usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/Cluster/api</code> for cluster and slot CRUD, identity, cluster-feature,
                    and slot-feature catalog management, single-well and fixed-platform filtering, batch export/restore,
                    and usage statistics, documented and explorable through the Swagger UI at
                    <code>/Cluster/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at <code>/Cluster/api/mcp</code> (and
                    WebSocket at <code>/Cluster/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing tools grouped as
                    <code>cluster_...</code>, <code>cluster_identity_...</code>,
                    <code>cluster_feature_category_...</code>, and <code>slot_feature_category_...</code> — every
                    non-statistics REST operation plus connectivity <code>ping</code> — with strict, closed
                    input/output JSON Schemas, so AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can query, create, update, and back
                    up clusters directly. Usage statistics stay REST-only.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the Cluster, catalog, backup/restore, and survey-display pages are
                    published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.Cluster.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other Blazor
                    applications — such as the Well and WellBore web apps — can embed the same cluster context and
                    unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
